# Implement mailbox change observation

## Goal

Wait for new or changed mail efficiently while preserving resumable ordering and
bounded resource use.

## Required work

- Define a provider-neutral change cursor and change-event envelope.
- Implement bounded wait, cancellation, timeout, reconnect, and backoff.
- Resume from the last acknowledged cursor and detect an invalid or expired
  cursor without silently skipping mailbox changes.
- Coalesce duplicate provider events without discarding distinct changes.
- Expose observation only while a client request or explicit service lifecycle
  owns it; do not create an implicit public daemon.

## Acceptance criteria

- Tests prove no duplicate or missing event across reconnect and cursor resume
  under the documented consistency model.
- A timed-out wait is a successful empty result, not an ambiguous failure.
- Cancellation releases connections and background work promptly.
- Event diagnostics contain identifiers and timing, never message content.

## Evidence

- Deterministic event-stream fixture
- Reconnect, duplicate, cursor-expiry, timeout, and cancellation tests

## Current implementation

- The web adapter now projects exact legacy core-v5 event responses into bounded
  opaque message/conversation IDs plus provider-neutral `created`, `updated`,
  and `deleted` classes. Provider actions `UPDATE`, `UPDATE_DRAFT`, and
  `UPDATE_FLAGS` normalize to `updated`; embedded message/conversation content,
  labels, and counts are not retained in the change envelope.
- Exact duplicate `(entity, action, ID)` changes are coalesced in stable
  response order while distinct actions for one ID remain distinct. One provider
  page is limited to 512 normalized changes; malformed IDs/actions fail closed
  and debug
  output redacts opaque IDs and event watermarks.
- `ObservedMailboxEventSequence` models the proven WebClients event-manager
  chain: each continuation must request the previous response `EventID`,
  `More=1` must advance the watermark, and `Refresh` invalidates resumable
  state. Current WebClients defines event refresh as a bitmask (`MAIL=1`,
  `CONTACTS=2`, `ALL=255`), so only a refresh containing the Mail bit
  invalidates
  mailbox resumability; contacts-only refresh does not. Sequences are capped at
  32 provider pages and 4096 distinct normalized changes, preserve provider
  order
  across pages, and coalesce exact duplicates across the chain.
- The classifier is now wired into a passive managed-browser wait. It enables
  CDP Network observation before listening, never injects a Proton event API
  request, accepts waits up to 30 seconds, treats a first-event timeout as a
  successful empty result, drains immediate `More=1` continuations, and disables
  Network before returning. The transport also caps unsolicited frames at 128
  and short waits temporarily lower the pipe read timeout so they do not
  overshoot the requested bound.
- Resume preflight now accepts an acknowledged provider watermark only when the
  browser-owned next core-v5 request starts at that exact watermark. A mismatch
  fails as a cursor gap, malformed watermarks fail before observation, and an
  empty timeout leaves the acknowledged watermark unchanged. The adapter still
  cannot seek an arbitrary older cursor after the browser event loop has moved.
- Provider-neutral cursor scope now binds opaque resume state to non-empty,
  bounded account, adapter, and adapter-generation identities with redacted
  diagnostics. Settled web event sequences can mint a scoped next cursor, and
  passive resume releases its provider watermark only for an exact current
  scope; account/adapter/generation drift fails before provider observation.
- Bounded waits now support cooperative cancellation through a cloneable
  handle. Cancellable waits poll the DevTools pipe at most every 25 ms, still
  disable Network and detach before surfacing cancellation, and synthetic
  coverage proves the same managed browser remains usable immediately after a
  cancelled wait.
- Bootstrap capture is now deterministic at the browser boundary. After proving
  the ready Mail shell, the driver enables bounded Network observation, reloads
  the same target, accepts exactly one completed
  `GET /api/core/v4/events/latest` lifecycle, projects only its bounded
  `EventID`, disables Network, and revalidates target origin. This is the exact
  initial promise current WebClients stores before its later v5 event poll; the
  adapter still never injects the provider API request itself.
- The captured bootstrap watermark binds directly into the same
  account/adapter/generation-scoped cursor type as a settled v5 sequence.
- Cursor failures now have provider-neutral contract classes before transport
  serialization exists. Malformed cursor state and account/adapter scope drift
  classify as `InvalidCursor`; adapter-generation drift, an exact-start gap, or
  a provider Mail refresh classify as `CursorExpired`. The web resume entry
  points surface those classes instead of leaking provider sequence errors.
- Initial no-cursor observation now keeps one Network capture active from the
  app-owned latest-event bootstrap through the first bounded v5 sequence. The
  provider-neutral `ObservedMailboxChangePage` returns ordered normalized
  changes plus a scoped next cursor; a first-v5 timeout is a successful empty
  page that carries the captured bootstrap cursor, while bootstrap/v5 start
  drift fails closed. Cancellation uses the same cleanup path.
- Resumed waits now expose the same change-page semantics. Exact scoped-cursor
  resume yields a settled sequence page with an advanced next cursor; a bounded
  timeout is a successful empty page carrying the exact acknowledged cursor.
  Invalid/expired cursor classification remains unchanged. Initial and resumed
  change-page waits both expose cooperative cancellation through the same
  Network-disable and target-detach cleanup path.
- Provider-owned reconnect/backoff is now compatible with passive observation.
  Current WebClients keeps the last EventID unchanged across failed calls and
  retries with its own increasing schedule. A tracked CDP `loadingFailed` now
  discards only that attempt and keeps the bounded observer alive for a later
  exact retry; the adapter never injects a retry or advances the cursor itself.
- Managed-browser lifetime now owns the web adapter generation. Every launch
  creates a fresh random 128-bit opaque generation token; event scopes bind the
  authenticated account plus the stable web-adapter ID to that generation.
  Synthetic process coverage proves same-generation use succeeds, account drift
  is invalid, and a cursor from a prior managed-browser launch becomes
  `CursorExpired` after restart. Generation diagnostics are redacted.
- Still required: choose and authenticate the public cursor serialization before
  advertising `wait_for_changes`.
